// NEGATIVE TEST — a by-value `self` is a COPY, and writing it is refused.
//
// This is the ORDINARY immutability rule rather than a new one, and that is the
// whole argument for refusing it. There is no `mut self` receiver form — `fn f(mut
// self)` is "Expected parameter name, but found 'self'" — so a by-value `self` is a
// binding that was never declared mutable, exactly like `let c` versus `let mut c`.
//
// MEASURED BEFORE THE REFUSAL EXISTED: this COMPILED, LINKED AND RAN. The write
// landed on the callee's copy, the caller observed nothing, and the program did not
// do what it said. That is the silent-wrong-result class this repository exists to
// refuse, and it is worse than a diagnostic, so the surface is refused rather than
// left parseable.
struct C {
    n: i64,
}

impl C {
    fn bump(self) -> i64 {
        self.n = self.n + 1;
        return self.n;
    }
}

fn main() {
    let c: C = C { n: 1 };
    print_int(c.bump());
}
